  *Abstract.* PlanetSimulation couples terrain, prescribed orbital motion, reflected illumination, and an optically active atmosphere in an interactive renderer. This paper explains the physical ideas behind those models and the approximations that make them practical. Particular attention goes to atmospheric density, molecular and aerosol extinction, single scattering, twilight visibility, and refractive ray curvature. For each major subsystem, we compare the implemented method with a plausible alternative and state what physical behavior is retained or lost. Twelve generated vector diagrams explain the algorithms; versioned captures show actual rendering. Historical README timings are distinguished from new matched Quadro M1000M and RTX 3070 Ti measurements, with recorded inputs and workload limits. The model is intentionally limited: it does not solve climate, fluid flow, multiple atmospheric scattering, or mutual N-body gravity.
]
#v(0.3cm)
*Keywords:* real-time graphics · procedural terrain · Keplerian dynamics · atmospheric optics · adaptive rendering · reproducible engineering

= Scope and physical questions

The paper describes the current source and its reproducible images, not a claim of physical fidelity for every planet. The useful questions are causal: what determines a surface point, what gravitational model moves the bodies, what radiance reaches the eye, and which terms can be approximated without destroying the intended visual behavior? Equations are simplified descriptions of the implementation; where a coefficient is tuned for the miniature scene, that is stated explicitly. Images come from `docs/screenshots/`, with capture inputs and hashes in `docs/captures/generation.json`. Diagrams in `figures/` are explanatory schematics generated by `make_figures.py`.

The atmosphere deserves special care. A convincing sunset depends on the length and density of the optical path, not merely on a sky color. The renderer therefore keeps absorption, out-scattering, in-scattering, and geometric visibility as separate quantities. Its reference pressure and temperature are configured inputs; no weather or climate equation evolves them. Tests and controlled captures check specific invariants and regressions, but an attractive image alone cannot establish that the numerical model is physically accurate.

= Representing a planet in a camera

== From configuration to a projected sphere

A position in a planet's local mesh is transformed by a model matrix, a camera view matrix, and a projection matrix. In the project's column-vector convention,

$ x_"clip" = P V M x_"local", quad x_"ndc" = x_"clip" / w_"clip" . $

This mapping establishes which physical coordinates each calculation uses. Heights belong to the rotating planet frame; orbital positions and light directions belong to the world frame; depth and pixel reconstruction belong to the camera frame. Mixing them can make a mountain move under a stationary observer or make a shadow follow the camera. GLM supplies the matrix operations, while behavior tests check the resulting projections. @fig:transform records the data path.

#figure(image("figures/early/transforms.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The foundational transform and shading path. `P V M` follows the current shader convention; the final display transform happens after linear-light composition. Generated schematic.]) <fig:transform>

A hidden-window `--render-test` and PNG smoke test make camera framing, framebuffer readback, and image output observable in headless environments. @fig:solar is an actual capture of a compact regression scene. It is evidence of the current renderer at a fixed input state.

#figure(image("../screenshots/solar-view.png", width: 82%), caption: [Headless rendering of the compact scenario. It checks the projected positions of the bodies at a reproducible input state. Source: `docs/screenshots/solar-view.png`; provenance: `docs/captures/generation.json`.]) <fig:solar>

== A reproducible scene as an experimental unit

The JSON parser performs type and range checks before GPU resources are replaced. Scene replay stores a resolved scenario, camera, time, and quality choices next to a PNG. The image is an observation; its sidecar specifies a repeatable input state. On live reload, a new CPU scene is staged before the current one is exchanged, so an invalid or half-written file retains the last valid scene. This matters for physical comparison: changing the camera or exposure at the same time as atmospheric density would confound the result.

= Terrain as a sampled physical surface

== Procedural surface as a camera-independent function

The surface height is evaluated in a normalized planet-local radial direction $u$. A simplified form is

$ h(u) = h_"landscape"(u) + sum_i A_i f_i(u; "seed"_i, "frequency"_i, "octaves"_i). $

The implemented functions include value-noise fBm and a ridged variant. Broad continent and cliff fields determine large-scale shape; finer octaves add local detail. This is synthetic topography: it has spatial correlation and multiple length scales but no tectonics, erosion, or material transport. The seed and local coordinate frame make repeated samples of the same point deterministic. Since terrain geometry is rebuilt as the camera moves, a view-dependent height function would make the ground itself rise and fall. The current code samples `heightAt(radial)` during every LOD rebuild. @fig:terrain distinguishes the fixed field from its variable triangulation.

#figure(image("figures/early/terrain.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The height field is fixed in planet coordinates; eight distance levels change density and apply a bounded inward offset to coarser regions. Bars show the working segment anchors and interpolated levels; the sphere is schematic. Generated schematic.]) <fig:terrain>

The sphere begins with 20 icosahedral faces, subdivided twice into 320 stable base faces. For nearby views, each base face is assigned one of eight edge-segment levels using its angular distance to the eye. A previous-zone hysteresis margin prevents repeated switching at a threshold. Steep faces can receive extra segments if the triangle budget permits. Edges shared by unequal zones are sampled once at the finer requirement, then reused on both sides; concentric interior rings fill the faces without T-junctions. The budget loop first removes optional steep refinement and then lowers less-important zones. This preserves one continuous sampled surface under a per-planet triangle ceiling.

The working segment sequence, from coarse to fine, is 3, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16. Existing base, middle and maximum settings anchor levels 0, 3 and 7. Seven surface-distance boundaries span 300 to 1000 m. The three-zone counts in older reports remain an aggregate (level 0, levels 1–6, level 7); captures now also print all eight counts.

Coarse samples receive an inward displacement $s = s_"max" ((n_"max" - n) / (n_"max" - n_"base"))^2$, clamped to zero for extra-steep detail. The configured `sink_depth_m` defaults to 1 m, additionally bounded to 0.1% of the radius remaining after maximum relief. Shared corners use the smallest incident offset; edges use the finer neighbor, with smooth interpolation toward corners and face centers. Shore refinement carries this scalar field through subdivision before displacement is applied. There is exactly one selected surface per direction, so coarse shells cannot poke through a simultaneously rendered fine shell. The sea uses the same eight-level selection with zero sinking. Geometry, shadows, reflections and foliage roots agree on the installed mesh.

This is a spatial sinking rule, not an animated LOD crossfade. Hysteresis and stable choices preserve vertices within unchanged levels; crossing a level can still move geometry. An offset below the sampled height also does not establish an error bound everywhere inside a triangle on arbitrary procedural relief. The accompanying `benchmarks/terrain-lod.md` records tests and controlled captures.

Two fixed before/after views (shoreline and grass) use all eight levels. Each already reaches the 100,000-triangle land budget and 60,000-triangle water budget, so this change redistributes detail rather than reducing those totals. Land uploads remain 12,000,000 bytes per rebuild; the sink scalar adds CPU metadata but no GPU vertex attribute or draw. The shoreline retains 19 finest base faces and the grass view retains 18. Capture timings overlapped compilation and are not used as performance evidence. Replay inputs, image pairs, logs and hashes are retained under `benchmarks/terrain-lod/`.

An alternative is a cube-sphere quadtree with screen-space error bounds. It can allocate triangles more directly to projected error and support very large planets, but needs explicit seam and level-transition rules. A fixed high-resolution sphere avoids LOD transitions at the cost of drawing distant detail that cannot affect the image. Here the icosahedral base and fixed height query suit a small interactive scene where local walking must remain stable.

The sampled height feeds normals and terrain color factors as well as eye clearance. Water is a separate spherical shell, an approximation that omits tides and waves in the body's shape. The local camera follows the planet's translation and rotation through a planet-fixed frame, so the same latitude/longitude refers to the same terrain location after orbital motion or spin. Walking near a pole uses a great-circle step and parallel transport of the view basis; longitude itself becomes singular there. @fig:pole illustrates why transporting the basis in 3-D is better behaved than rebuilding it from longitude after every step.

#figure(image("figures/early/pole.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Great-circle position update and basis transport across the north pole. A longitude-derived frame can flip at the pole; a transported 3-D basis remains continuous. Generated schematic.]) <fig:pole>

#figure(image("../screenshots/surface-view.png", width: 83%), caption: [Surface-camera capture of procedurally generated terrain. Geometry derives from the stable height function while tessellation is selected for this camera. Source: current gallery.]) <fig:surface>

== GPU field evaluation and planned surface ownership

GPU field evaluation and complete consumer ownership now support opt-in captures; interactive acceptance remains pending. The starting source audit found CPU bulk height/gradient/material sampling and duplicated land vertices, CPU shoreline subdivision and grass planning, but already GPU density-column tables and atmospheric ray integration. The migration retains the 320 triangular icosahedral panels: CPU workers select topology, reconcile shared edges and choose inward sink scalars; GPU compute evaluates configured planet-local fields into reusable buffers. Hexagonal duals require pentagonal exceptions on a sphere, while H3 child boundaries only approximately fit parents. The checked design worksheet confirms twelve exceptional dual cells and manifold closure through three subdivision levels. A cube sphere is another possible future chart system, but changing topology would also change current face/replay identities.

#figure(grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 8pt,
  block(fill: pale, inset: 9pt, radius: 4pt)[*CPU ownership* #linebreak()Validated parameters and double body transforms #linebreak()Canonical topology, LOD and sink scalars #linebreak()Sparse matching collision triangles #linebreak()Memory ledger and versioned installation],
  block(fill: pale, inset: 9pt, radius: 4pt)[*GPU ownership* #linebreak()Bulk shape, gradient and material fields #linebreak()Resident terrain and water buffers #linebreak()Grass metadata, roots and draw queues #linebreak()Atmospheric fields and integration]),
  caption: [Generation contract. One complete generation supplies main views, shadows, reflections, foliage and contacts. Capture follows this contract; interactive compute acceptance remains pending and CPU fallback stays available.])

The current land payload is 120 bytes per triangle. Assuming a closed, canonically indexed 100,000-triangle surface with 50,002 shared vertices, 32-byte double radial/sink descriptors plus indices would upload 2,800,064 bytes instead of 12,000,000: an analytical 76.666% reduction before parameters, metadata, alignment and staging. GPU outputs and spare generations still occupy memory; this estimate is neither a measured speedup nor a total VRAM saving. A bounded CPU collision mirror must intersect the same selected triangle planes and inward offsets as rendering, avoiding full-body GPU readback. The initial grass planner depended on CPU shaped vertices; T3b2 below removes that render mirror from new compute captures.

The plan keeps the existing GPU atmosphere table and optical integration, caches small uniform coefficient packs, and identifies the readback/sort of highlight tiles as a remaining reduction candidate. Future foliage budgeting reserves configured density in a near plateau before shrinking a Gaussian shoulder under measured memory/work limits; impossible near budgets require a stated fallback rather than an unachievable density promise. Backend/field/topology keys, canonical seams, double noise-coordinate arithmetic, GL resource limits, exact locked replay, sparse-contact coverage and atomic land/water/grass publication are prerequisite gates. GL 4.3 compute is opt-in until parity and total-cost measurements pass; GL 3.3 retains CPU terrain. Detailed ownership, panel comparisons, phased tasks and acceptance targets are in `architecture/terrain-gpu/plan.md`.

The first prerequisite (T1) separates a read-only CPU field oracle with a 704-byte parameter pack from indexed 32-byte radial/sink inputs. A bounded exact-coordinate height cache and validated field/topology/backend keys expose planning and bulk-evaluation work. Seventeen independent pre-refactor mesh snapshots retain exact vertex, normal, color, index and sink bytes, including both poles and production views. The implementation and validation are in `architecture/terrain-gpu/contracts/study.md`.

The compute proof (T2) generates double fields and legacy draw buffers on GL 4.3. A 100,000-triangle generation uploads 2,800,880 input bytes including parameters and controls instead of 12,000,000 shaped-mesh bytes (76.66% less). Packing, poles, extreme seeds, mixed LOD, shoreline seams, winding, replay and GL 3.3 fallback are checked. Land and water complete before publication. T3a now uses a radial topology index and lazy 1,024-position oracle cache for matching triangle-plane contacts, preserving float rounding and sinking without full mesh scans or GPU readbacks. CPU subdivision remains; grass compatibility vectors were retained through T3b1. Interactive compute awaits asynchronous consumer publication and hardware cost gates; no total-frame speedup is claimed. Studies are in `architecture/terrain-gpu/compute/study.md` and `contacts/study.md`.

T3b1 generates resident 64-byte grass triangle descriptors from the existing compute terrain buffers: original IDs, double bounds, conservative biome eligibility and Gaussian-weighted area. A 100,000-triangle oracle uploads 176 bytes and retains 6,400,160 additional bytes; native weighted-area error is at most 2.21e-14 on llvmpipe. Renderer readback and cached uploads are zero. Queried limits, chunking, buffer sizes, generation keys and GL range restoration are checked. At T3b1, CPU slot allocation/render vectors were retained; that prerequisite added work without claiming a frame speedup. The study and evidence are in `architecture/terrain-gpu/grass-metadata/`.

T3b2 now computes budgeted hash selection, rounded-slot density search and deterministic group/bucket prefixes on GPU. Fresh compute captures use `gpu-v1` and omit full CPU land/water render vectors and bulk field evaluation; older replays retain CPU planning. A 100,000-triangle case uploads 2,840 metadata/allocation bytes and reads one 224-byte capture summary, submitting 1,048,429 candidates within a conservative queried limit. Triangle/seed/rank root identities remain stable. No hardware frame improvement is claimed; T3c still owns asynchronous complete consumer installation. The implementation and validation are in `architecture/terrain-gpu/grass-allocation/`.

T3c1 introduces one persistent CPU executor with one replaceable queued snapshot and one completion slot. Requests own field/settings/topology inputs and carry epoch, body, field, mode and serial identities. The worker constructs canonical compute land/water topology and matching sparse contacts without bulk render evaluation. Reload invalidates old work without joining; shutdown joins before trace/context ownership ends. Normal CPU walking polls completed work and rejects incompatible or older generations. Compute capture explicitly waits for CPU preparation and retains its GPU waits; complete asynchronous GPU/grass publication remains T3c2/T3c3. Contact ownership temporarily duplicates canonical CPU topology during staging, so this checkpoint does not claim lower total memory or a hardware speedup. The study is in `architecture/terrain-gpu/async/worker/`.

T3c2 adds owned land/water and resident-grass preparation. GPU command order carries terrain-to-metadata-to-allocation dependencies without CPU fence waits during submission. Polling uses zero-timeout fences and retrieves one completed 224-byte allocation summary, validating prefixes, slot caps and candidate totals before preallocating texture views, blade queues, indirect commands and VAOs. A separate resource fence precedes patch commit. An initial 512 MiB logical ceiling reserves terrain and worst-case grass buffers; it does not estimate driver physical memory or available VRAM. Captures use explicit waits through these APIs at their original planning eye. Atomic scene publication, retirement and interactive opt-in remain T3c3/T3c4. The study is in `architecture/terrain-gpu/async/gpu/`.

T3c3a adds a complete transaction owner with off-live land/water/grass/contact resources, separate terrain and grass planning anchors, pre-dispatch overlap admission and no-throw publication transfers. One GPU preparation globally and one preparing/retiring spare per body bound overlap; default logical limits are 512 MiB per set and 1 GiB aggregate. Publication validates request and live consumer identities, creates a last-use fence before transfer, then keeps all old resources together until zero-timeout retirement polling signals completion. Disabled water and grass replace prior consumers at the same boundary. This owner is not yet connected to renderer frame boundaries or reload: T3c3b/T3c3c must refresh dependent caches and preserve the old scene through replacement readiness. Interactive compute remains gated. The study is in `architecture/terrain-gpu/async/publication/`.

T3c3b connects complete resident capture consumers to the pre-character frame boundary. CPU worker outputs stay off live resources while a scoped planning preview derives the exact chase eye from prospective sparse contacts. It restores motion, contact caches, cameras, input presses and replay state before GPU submission; trails and exhaust advance only in the real step after publication. Grass-only replacement exchanges the patch and receipt without replacing terrain or contacts. Changed land invalidates shadows and every publication invalidates whole-frame reuse. Managed scene passes validate keys/revisions before draws, skip independent patch preparation and expose consumed main, shadow, reflection, grass and water revisions. Capture waits remain explicit; transactional scene reload and asynchronous interactive acceptance remain T3c3c/T3c4. The study is in `architecture/terrain-gpu/async/renderer/`.

T3c3c1 adds a whole-scene replacement owner. A validated off-live CPU scene/config owns resident terrain, water, grass and contacts for every body. One exclusive replacement/retirement lease prevents competing terrain submission; aggregate admission charges old scene sets before replacement dispatch. Complete readiness and unchanged request/live identities precede a last-use fence and no-throw exchange of scene/config, mesh arrays, grass/trail state and receipts. Old resources remain owned and charged through zero-timeout retirement, including failed polls. Body reordering/count changes travel with their own fields and mounted cameras. Renderer reload integration and movement/body-switch acceptance remain T3c3c2/T3c3c3; interactive compute remains gated. The study is in `architecture/terrain-gpu/async/scene/`.

T3c3c2 connects the owner to Renderer::reload() and shared R/file-watch handling. A fresh config/replay snapshot and preallocated tracking arrays accompany all-body terrain/contact/grass preparation. Exclusive future-epoch CPU builds reuse the same executor without changing the live epoch on failure. Scoped prospective character previews restore old state; exact body-local replay anchors avoid coordinate round-trip rounding, and trail/exhaust data are validated before exchange. The complete boundary swaps tracking and replay/options, rebinds cameras/contacts, resets character/effects and invalidates shadow/reflection/frame/orbit caches. CPU and legacy adapters stage the same consumers and fence old resources. One retired scene remains owned until last-use completion; captures wait explicitly, interactive CPU retirement polls without a timeout. Backend/planner, dimensions and renderer quality pipeline changes are rejected. Movement/body-switch/Moon acceptance remains T3c3c3 and interactive compute stays gated. The study is in `architecture/terrain-gpu/async/reload/`.

