A best-effort 20 FPS quality target uses a smoothed frame estimate $m_t = 0.85 m_(t-1) + 0.15 f_t$ to select one of six scene-resolution scales, from 100% down to 25%. After a 1.5-second cooldown, sustained times above 55 ms lower resolution; times below 32 ms permit recovery. Only moving interactive scenes feed the controller. A graphics-memory estimate can cap the initial scale, reserving most reported memory for other uses; fixed-resolution captures remain deterministic. The HUD stays at native window resolution. @fig:adaptive shows the control law, not a measured response trace. This is a rendering control, not a change to the atmosphere's physical parameters. Its lower resolution may still lose thin clouds or distant silhouettes; a strict 20 FPS guarantee is impossible once the minimum scale is reached or scene cost is unrelated to pixel count.

